activity-date.narrative:
This humanitarian program is proposing to focus on the treatment of SAM of children under the age of five in areas with a high prevalence of malnutrition. It will be deployed in a two-fold approach: 
1) Through emergency rapid interventions in acute nutrition crises and ;
2) By launching the second phase of a three-year approach to improve the nutrition and health status of children under the age of five as well as PLW by addressing the consequences of the nutrition crisis in areas identified by the Consortium
Across the whole of DRC, the Consortium will continue its work with the SNSAP and PRONANUT to improve the identification of provinces and health zones in high priority need for humanitarian interventions (GAM > 10% and/or SAM > 2% and/or aggravating factors). (See part 4 for more details).

The SNSAP is based on the monitoring of routine nutritional indicators collected in health centre sentinel sites triangulated with data from other sectors such as agriculture, economy (prices), epidemiological surveillance, hygiene, water and sanitation to help comprehend globally the nutritional situation of a given health zone. For every health zone in alert, a meeting with other nutrition coordinating agencies will be held to decide if it is required to conduct a SMART survey in order to evaluate the severity of the nutritional situation. If severity is confirmed, the PUNCH will launch a 6-month intervention in health zones where needs are the greatest by implementing the following activities :
	Support to health facilities for the provision of inpatient and outpatient treatment of SAM : Primary Health Care to all children under the age of five including the provision of Ready-to-use Therapeutic Foods (RUTF), medicines, as well as anthropometric materials ;
	Provision of equipment and drugs for PLW;
	Water and Sanitation support for health centres ;
	Scaled-up community mobilisation including the training of families on how to diagnose acute malnutrition on children with MUAC tapes (Family MUAC).
The PUNCH Phase 2 will continue to ensure multi-year support in three different identified provinces, which will support a more adequate exit strategy. As the program has been designed with a 3-year rationale, a second phase covering 12 months of program implementation is scheduled for March 1st 2022 - as the first phase is ending in February 28th, 2022. 

In Kasai Oriental, Haut Lomami and Mai Ndombe, activities in the six health zones will continue to ensure:
•	Access to, and quality of the management of SAM ; 
•	Reduction of child mortality and morbidity, with support to primary health care for children under the age of five ;
•	Increased access to, and quality of, antenatal and postnatal care services for PLW including assisting delivery ; 
•	Increased access to informed provision of modern family planning methods with a focus on women coming to postnatal care. 

This program will maintain a specific focus on nutrition while ensuring that basic health interventions are available and delivered at health centres - by providing support to prevention, diagnosis and treatment of diseases, very often associated with acute malnutrition and ensuring the continuum of care. SAM management will be integrated into health and mainstreamed as part of the basic package of health services, breaking the inter-generation undernutrition circle through mother and child health care. 

As identified with the communities and through needs assessment, the program will continue its support to health facilities with basic WASH supplies at the health facility level this includes the monthly provision of soap as well as handwashing stations and cleaning supplies. However, in Kamina, as ALIMA is launching SRH activities during this second phase, construction or rehabilitation of WASH infrastructures will be necessary to reinforce infection prevention and control in some health centers.

A stronger emphasis will also be put on community engagement to ensure interventions are tailored to the specific needs of communities. This will include support to existing community structures for the detection of acute malnutrition with Family MUAC and adapted behaviour change activities focused on Infant and Young Child Feeding (IYCF), vaccination, key family actions, hygiene, dietary diversity, use of sexual and reproductive health (SRH) services including family planning, treatment seeking and dangers signs.
